  in signal here  uh from you know almost close to  baseline to this really robust response  and that actually increased our  sensitivity by a hundred fold  and so it was just really exciting to  see this it's kind of you know these  moments in science where  a lot of times things don't work the  first time and second time and third  time but then when they do work that one  time it's just a really incredible  feeling  and so we were able ultimately to sort  of in addition incorporate other  improvements to get to a sensitivity  that was sort of clinically useful which  is  at a molar concentration rate that's  about 30 or 30 copies per microliter of  sars kobe 2 rna in about 20 minutes  and in addition we since we have an  on-campus or testing lab  we're able to actually take some samples  that were left over  from their lab and see if we could use  our assay  to detect sarcoby to rna that were  actually from patient samples  and what you can see here so i won't go  to too much detail but this black line  is essentially we're looking for a  difference between this black line of  the pink line  and you can see here that you can see  this above the pink line which means  that it's positive  and again for here this is actually with  a lower viral load  so this number means that there's less  spiral particles there or rna there  and it's still above but it's closer to  the baseline  on the other hand though negative  samples all show that it was below this  pink uh baseline here so essentially we  could distinguish between positive and  negative samples  uh at this range of ct values between 8  and 29 which  correspond to some of the early  infectious stages of coping 19 and  that's really  nice for us because essentially when you  want to control a pandemic  it's not necessarily you have to have  the absolute most  sensitive tests you know in the world  actually what's more  important in some cases is be able to  frequently test and catch people at the  most infectious stages when they're most  likely to spread it to other people  and so it's really nice for us to see  that in this range between  less than 29 where most um positives in  this kind of group were  we could actually able we were able to  detect them with this uh  technology that we had developed  and so just to kind of conclude um  basically we found that these  unrelated crispr nucleuses were from  different sort of  bacteria different biological  systems could be really combined  together for fast and sensitive rna  detection  and that makes it that simplicity also  being this kind of a single  uh reaction makes it really amenable for  rapid testing  which is often for this point of care  diagnosis diagnosis  um compared to for example traditional  approaches which  require you know centralized lab a lot  of equipment trained personnel  and i think just to end with i think  again this sort of  goes toward the point that if you have  an understanding of how the molecules  work to drive a process in life  that can really lead to innovations that  are valuable  in biology research and medicine that's  not just true for this but also you can  see in  my mentor jennifer doudna's work on  crispr that you know crispr is a natural  bacterial system nobody ever thought  you know it was not designed for genome  editing but nowadays  uh it's been widely adopted for for  genome manipulation  um and so i think i have to end by  really thanking the mbmb program for  really  instilling that appreciation for  molecular events and also stars to  for their support and  of course mentors lynn regan jennifer  doudna and tom rappaport  and also the great endowment lab for for  their help and happy to talk more at the  breakout sessions  thank you very much tina our last alumni  speaker  today is elias curano he's a current md  phd student at yale medical school  and my stars one graduate coordinator  elias  hi everyone uh thanks for having me  today  my name is elias i'm a as dean chang  mentioned currently an md phd student at  yale but uh  graduated from yale a long time ago in  2012  um and you know i've been really  fortunate to be part of the stars  program  you know i remember my first day at yale  just feeling completely out of place you  know so i'm i'm  from a small town in florida hialeah  florida it's a really small city  in miami and growing up just like you  know in a spanish-speaking community  with spanish-speaking parents  you know i i never felt out of place i  always felt like i was home  and when i got to yale that feeling of  belonging was immediately  uh swept away from me and i immediately  fell out of place  and i think many of you here probably  share  with some of that feeling um and i  remember  you know even the smallest things it  made me feel like a foreigner  uh so one big thing in florida is  wearing these uh  these silk shirts these guys and uh i  remember wearing one probably my first  week  at yale and you know one of my floor  mates made you know some sort of uh  joke about about my shirt and i just  remember  immediately feeling like it didn't  belong here and i think  uh you know early grades you know my  first c at yale  uh uh compounded that feeling of not  belonging at yale feeling like  like i didn't belong and really you know  that first year i felt really lost and i  think  serving currently as the graduate  student coordinator for stars one  many students feel this way and and it  really doesn't matter what your  background is or where you're from and  it's really hard to feel like you belong  here especially if you know you're from  a  underrepresented community or a first  generation college student  it's really easy to feel lost and feel  like you don't have a community here at  yale  um but i remember my sophomore year i uh  interacted with dean garcia at la casa  and uh she really pointed me to the  stars program and  really pointed me to people who made me  feel like like i did belong at yale  and she was one of the first people who  really introduced me to the program and  introduced me to the faculty members  that were part of stars that had sort of  voluntarily chosen to mentor students  like myself which  was really remarkable uh to me at the  time uh  and i think like many of you have  mentioned you know dr black was just  uh an absolute force i mean  you could sort of be failing and and  messing up and not be on time for  something and she was after you she was  pushing you she was asking why you  weren't showing up to  review sessions you know i remember one  time i at the time when i was a student  she was hosting these nightly review  sessions for chemistry and  i think i missed it once and she's like  why aren't you here you're the student  who belongs here the most you need the  most help  and you know it was that level of  intensity and that level of like  sort of looking out for students and  chasing students down that i think is  really really remarkable and really  made a dr black stand out among all of  the faculty members here you know she  really uh  chased after you um and the same was  true for dr moreno so  you know i ended up doing star summer  and dr moreno  had a similar energy and a similar force  but you know she really taught me  how to succeed in science and how to  succeed uh in stem and  and that to me was a really uh really  remarkable just having someone who was  so accomplished in her own field in her  own right  take the time to mentor me and shepherd  me and  just teach me what i needed to know uh  from you know how to write an email that  was appropriate to a professor  uh to how to design a slide that didn't  look like the example we saw a little  bit earlier and i know  we all make jokes about that but it's  true i mean  you know the number one thing you're  doing as a graduate student or someone  in stem or  in any field is communicating and being  able to communicate effectively and  clearly is  just so invaluable um and it's a bit of  a softer skill but one that i really  credit uh stars with  you know you know i think stars for me  was was really the first time that  you know not only was i taught how to  succeed but i was also taught to  never give up you know i i think deep  down inside you know i always knew i  wanted to pursue stem  i you know i didn't have parents that  that um you know were scientists or went  to college for that matter  but you know the stars faculty members  really taught me uh to kind of pursue  that and and  never give up on that and really seek  mentors uh that could help me  along the way and um you know that was  extremely  invaluable for me so since that time as  a stars  uh student i you know i ended up doing  star summer as well as stars too  um one thing i'll mention that someone  actually said a little earlier today  is that in addition to the mentorship  one  key piece of the stars program was the  financial support that i received so  at the time when i was doing stars 1 and  stars 2  i had the opportunity to go to multiple  conferences and present multiple  posters and talks and it was because the  stars program paid for my lodging and  paid for my registration i mean  there was absolutely no way i was going  to be able to take advantage of those  opportunities  if stars wasn't financing those  opportunities for me and that was really  really invaluable  even with star skew you know the  opportunity to get paid to do research  to both further your  intellectual development and your  research skills while also  generating the income that perhaps you  need because you're sending money home  or you're paying for your own books or  you know paying for your own  day-to-day expenses it's really  something that you know i i sort of look  back and i think many of us feel the  same way  you know i really credit stars with sort  of saving me  uh in many ways and putting me on the  right track um  but since that time i i went on to start  an md phd program at yale thankfully  to most of my stars mentors including  dean chang um and you know have recently  started my own biotech uh and just  closed our series a  so you know i say that not to really  brag but just to say that you know i  went from getting  c's and chemistry and you know virtually  failing  out of out of the university uh to now  achieving some level of success and  it really wasn't something i did on my  own it was really something i did  because of stars and through the support  i received from star so  you know i'm just really grateful uh to  the students i've been able to mentor  through stars but  especially to the individuals who have  mentored me i mean it's really been an  invaluable  process and experience for myself and  you know i'm just really grateful uh to  be here with you all and  to see that uh so many people benefited  from this program and  uh decided to give back by being here  today so thank you all and uh thank you  dean chang for organizing all of this  and  putting us all together thank you very  much elias and thank you to all the  speakers in the morning session
 

